# flow — /flow, start to finish

This rule carries **only the trigger**: when running `/flow`, load the pipeline,
`skills/flow-contracts/pipeline.md`, first, before any other step. Never act on a remembered
version of it — a command that skips the load is running on a stale copy of the state machine.

<!-- /core -->

## The pipeline at a glance

When running `/flow`, follow this **fixed three-state pipeline**:

```text
/flow  (no state)          → STARTED                  you: /clear, then /flow <name> to implement
/flow  (STARTED, planned)  → IN_PROGRESS or FINISHED  lands straight away unless a question is open
/flow  <fix instructions>  → IN_PROGRESS or FINISHED  lands straight away unless a question is open
/flow  (bare, IN_PROGRESS) → IN_PROGRESS or FINISHED  terminal only on the merge-and-push route
```

One invocation carries the run named after wherever it stops. A run that writes `IN_PROGRESS`
continues into integrate in the same invocation unless its `Decisions:` carries a `?`, which stops
it with the handoff block. The human gate is a property of the
state, not a separate step — which is why no command exists whose only job is to record that a
review happened. A creating or fix run produces the staged diff, so reviewing and testing are one gate. Every invocation is re-entrant, and a fix never moves the state.

A bare invocation at `IN_PROGRESS` lands the branch by the project's `## default landing route`, and
asks only when none is declared: open a PR by default, merge and push, or leave it to you. Choosing merge-and-push continues the same invocation once the branch is
merged, syncing, dropping the change directory, pushing and removing the worktrees; open PR and manual stop and hand off
instead.

## What the pipeline file holds

**States** and **State transitions** (`skills/flow-contracts/pipeline.md`) — **load that file
first**, before any other step of `/flow`. It is canonical for the state
definitions, the command→state transition table, the wrong-state handoff, the
handoff output shape. The finish contract itself is split by run:
`skills/flow-contracts/finish-contract-run1.md`, canonical for run 1 and loaded by `/flow`'s
integrate phase alone, and `skills/flow-contracts/finish-contract-run2.md`, canonical for run 2
and loaded by `/flow`'s cleanup phase alone.

Narrower contracts sit beside it, indexed once in `skills/flow-contracts/SKILL.md`; load the one a
step actually needs, not the directory. The reasoning behind the contracts — why an ordering is what
it is, what was rejected, what was measured — lives in `docs/rationale/`, for whoever *edits* a
contract.

These files are installed alongside the skills, so the paths above resolve
wherever this rule is read.
